Hi!

The reason for this is that the 'sh' executes the 'while' command in a new shell
where another variable 'rand' is defined. Returning from this subshell, the
variable 'rand' inside the 'while-shell' is lost and the old variable 'rand'
appears again.

You might try '/bin/bash' instead of 'sh'.
In the bash a similar behavior can be observed. The bash opens a subshell too
when piping something to the while command. To avoid piping I've found a way,
which satisfys the bash: I feed it from its 'backside'. (I'm sorry for this
idiom)

Instead of
	ps -e -o pid | while read; do .... done
write
	ps -e -o pid > numbers.tmp
	while read; do ... done < numbers.tmp
This will do the job.

The whole example will then look


#! /bin/bash
rand=`expr 1`
/bin/ps -e -o pid > numbers.tmp
while read mypid
do
if [ "${mypid}" != "PID" ]; then
                rand=`expr \( "${rand}" + "${mypid}" \) % 100`
        fi
        echo "inside loop: rand=" ${rand}
done < numbers.tmp
echo "outside loop: rand=" ${rand}


Best regards,
	Robert
-- 

